<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henan is a province of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> on the middle and lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhengzhou">Zhengzhou</a> as its capital. Often called the birthplace of Chinese civilisation, it holds the Erlitou site, the Shang capitals at Zhengzhou and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anyang">Anyang</a>, and the old imperial capitals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>. Many states of the Zhou period lay within it, and it remains one of China's most populous provinces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henan.jpg" alt="Henan" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shang_Inscribed_Ox_Scapula_(for_divination).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Henan means south of the river, the river being the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, though changes in its course and in administrative boundaries mean that about a quarter of the province now lies north of it. The province is also abbreviated Yu, after Yuzhou, the name of the region in the ancient system of nine provinces. Most of it forms part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a>, called the breadbasket of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, with floodplains in the east and mountains in the west. The Yellow River enters from the north-west, and its silt has raised the river bed and caused floods that shaped the region. Mount <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and its Shaolin Temple lie near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhengzhou">Zhengzhou</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Prehistoric cultures such as the Yangshao and Longshan were active in what is now northern Henan. The later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erlitou-culture">Erlitou culture</a> has been controversially identified with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xia-dynasty">Xia dynasty</a>. The Shang dynasty, from about 1600 to about 1046 BCE, had its capitals in the province, and the oracle bone inscriptions found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yinxu">Yinxu</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anyang">Anyang</a> give insight into its writing, rituals and social structure. After the Zhou overthrew the Shang at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muye">Battle of Muye</a>, the political centre moved away from Henan for the first time. Under the Han, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> became the political and cultural heart of the empire.</p><p>Henan passed repeatedly between rival regimes from 220 to 589, yet remained an agricultural base. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang of Sui</a> had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a> extended to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>. In the Five Dynasties period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> became the capital of four dynasties, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, who reunified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 982, Kaifeng overtook Luoyang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> as the largest city in China and in the world. In 1127 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a> captured Kaifeng and took the Song emperor prisoner, and in 1194 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> changed course, displacing millions.</p><p>After the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> in 1641, his forces besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> three times. During the third, Ming troops breached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> dikes to flood the rebels. The water submerged the city, and of its 378,000 residents only 30,000 survived. A dike breach at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhengzhou">Zhengzhou</a> on 30 September 1887 killed over two million people by one account, with estimates from 930,000 to 7 million. In 1938 the government led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> bombed the Huayuankou dam to halt the Japanese advance, causing floods that killed hundreds of thousands. A famine followed in 1942–43.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Henan is a province of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhengzhou">Zhengzhou</a> as its capital. Four historical capitals of China, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anyang">Anyang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> and Zhengzhou, lie within it. It has a population of over 99 million, the third largest of any province and the largest among inland provinces. The capital moved from Kaifeng to Zhengzhou in 1954 because of the city's importance for rail transport.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 422–423 Emperor Mingyuan took Luoyang, Xuchang and Hulao and most of <b>Henan</b> and western Shandong; Emperor Wen's great invasion of 450 was lured across the Yellow River, flanked and destroyed, and Wei armies laid waste the provinces to the south before withdrawing in January 451.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-wei">Northern Wei · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>King Pan Geng is credited with moving the capital to Yin, near modern Anyang in <b>Henan</b>, around 1300 BCE, and the inscriptions found there, which begin in the reign of Wu Ding in the thirteenth century BCE, make the late Shang the first period of Chinese history documented…</blockquote><a href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1259 Mongol armies attacked in Sichuan, Shandong, <b>Henan</b> and Guangxi, and Möngke Khan died on 11 August during the siege of Diaoyu Castle in Chongqing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-song">Southern Song · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Its remnants scattered to western Sichuan, northern Tibet and to <b>Henan</b> and Hebei, where they persisted into the middle of the Ming.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-xia">Western Xia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Erlitou culture was an early Bronze Age society and archaeological culture of the Yellow River valley, named after the site of Erlitou in Yanshi, <b>Henan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/erlitou-culture">Erlitou culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>A 2021 study found that millet farmers from <b>Henan</b> and Shandong helped to form the Hongshan people, moving north by inland and coastal routes, and a 2025 study modelled them as a mixture of local Ancient Northeast Asian ancestry, probably from the Zhaobaogou culture, and that of Dawenkou-related…</blockquote><a href="/culture/hongshan-culture">Hongshan culture · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
